event: response.function_call_arguments.done
data: {"type":"response.function_call_arguments.done","sequence_number":534,"item_id":"fc_0aa41dd63d0397f1016adb485bfacc819192ff85885cc249a6","output_index":1,"arguments":"{\"artifact\":{\"base_candidate\":1,\"patches\":[{\"op\":\"add\",\"path\":\"/graph/inferences/0/argument/0/uses/-\",\"value\":\"M008\"},{\"op\":\"replace\",\"path\":\"/graph/inferences/0/argument/0/text\",\"value\":\"Let D=dim_H(E) and F=E×[0,1] with the stipulated concatenated Euclidean metric. First, 0≤D≤n<∞: for n=0 the ambient space is a singleton. For n>0, compactness gives a bounded box containing E; cover the box by a grid of cubes of side h. For 0<h≤1 the number is at most C h^(−n), each cube has diameter at most √n h, and for every m>n their total m-power diameter cost tends to zero. This local covering argument establishes D≤n by the definition of Hausdorff dimension. For the upper bound fix q>D. M003 gives H^q(E)=0. Use the exact Nat-indexed scale-cover formula M008; it has no normalization factor. If n=0, E is the singleton {0} in ℝ^0, so cover F by intervals of length tending to zero; their (q+1)-cost tends to zero. If n>0, the zero-measure conclusion for E and M008 give arbitrarily small q-cost covers at each positive scale. Replace zero-diameter nonempty members by small open balls with total additional q-cost arbitrarily small. Partition [0,1] into at most 1/r_j+1 intervals of lengths at most r_j for each positive diameter r_j≤1. Products have diameter at most √2 r_j, and total (q+1)-cost is bounded by 2(√2)^(q+1)Σ r_j^q, tending to zero. Thus H^(q+1)(F)=0. The zero-measure-to-dimension consequence follows from the dimension definition (equivalently the source threshold characterization). Let q decrease to D to obtain dim_H(F)≤D+1.\"},{\"op\":\"add\",\"path\":\"/graph/inferences/0/applications/-\",\"value\":{\"input\":\"M008\",\"at\":\"S001\",\"bindings\":\"Instantiate with the Euclidean ambient space and A=E or F, exponent q or q+1, and positive scale δ; identify the formula's cover infimum with the scale content in the argument.\",\"hypothesis_checks\":[\"The ambient space has extended metric, measurable structure and Borel structure.\"]}}]}}"}

event: response.output_item.done
data: {"type":"response.output_item.done","sequence_number":535,"output_index":1,"item":{"type":"function_call","id":"fc_0aa41dd63d0397f1016adb485bfacc819192ff85885cc249a6","call_id":"call_9df566e4f9a2409daa76ff76c2e69f27","name":"submit_draft","arguments":"{\"artifact\":{\"base_candidate\":1,\"patches\":[{\"op\":\"add\",\"path\":\"/graph/inferences/0/argument/0/uses/-\",\"value\":\"M008\"},{\"op\":\"replace\",\"path\":\"/graph/inferences/0/argument/0/text\",\"value\":\"Let D=dim_H(E) and F=E×[0,1] with the stipulated concatenated Euclidean metric. First, 0≤D≤n<∞: for n=0 the ambient space is a singleton. For n>0, compactness gives a bounded box containing E; cover the box by a grid of cubes of side h. For 0<h≤1 the number is at most C h^(−n), each cube has diameter at most √n h, and for every m>n their total m-power diameter cost tends to zero. This local covering argument establishes D≤n by the definition of Hausdorff dimension. For the upper bound fix q>D. M003 gives H^q(E)=0. Use the exact Nat-indexed scale-cover formula M008; it has no normalization factor. If n=0, E is the singleton {0} in ℝ^0, so cover F by intervals of length tending to zero; their (q+1)-cost tends to zero. If n>0, the zero-measure conclusion for E and M008 give arbitrarily small q-cost covers at each positive scale. Replace zero-diameter nonempty members by small open balls with total additional q-cost arbitrarily small. Partition [0,1] into at most 1/r_j+1 intervals of lengths at most r_j for each positive diameter r_j≤1. Products have diameter at most √2 r_j, and total (q+1)-cost is bounded by 2(√2)^(q+1)Σ r_j^q, tending to zero. Thus H^(q+1)(F)=0. The zero-measure-to-dimension consequence follows from the dimension definition (equivalently the source threshold characterization). Let q decrease to D to obtain dim_H(F)≤D+1.\"},{\"op\":\"add\",\"path\":\"/graph/inferences/0/applications/-\",\"value\":{\"input\":\"M008\",\"at\":\"S001\",\"bindings\":\"Instantiate with the Euclidean ambient space and A=E or F, exponent q or q+1, and positive scale δ; identify the formula's cover infimum with the scale content in the argument.\",\"hypothesis_checks\":[\"The ambient space has extended metric, measurable structure and Borel structure.\"]}}]}}","status":"completed"}}

